WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.740
STEIN: Good morning.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:07.030
The subject of the morning session is "Public
Opinion and the 2016 Election."

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:13.749
The Council has held similar meetings in prior
election seasons, but I think in a minute

00:00:13.749 --> 00:00:17.200
or two you'll see why this session might be
a little bit different.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:26.030
Our three panelists are Scott Miller, in the
middle, who has been doing political communications

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.910
and strategy for more than 40 years.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.189
His clients-
MILLER: Way more.

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:31.189
(Laughs.)

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:37.870
STEIN: -during that period have been-included
Senator Gary Hart, Michael Dukakis, the Bush-Cheney

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:44.350
campaign, Ross Perot, as well as Vice President
Mondale and candidates around the world.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:50.350
To my immediate left is Kellyanne Conway,
who has worked for the Republican Party and

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:56.150
Republican candidates over the years, including
President Reagan, Speaker Gingrich, Vice President

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:57.820
Quayle, and Jack Kemp.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:05.050
And to the far left is Doug Schoen of Douglas
Schoen LLC, who is most well-known for his

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.330
work for President Clinton and for Mayor Bloomberg.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:15.990
They are among the most experienced polling
and political consulting figures in the U.S.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:21.930
What's different this-for this session, I
think, is this: Kellyanne is-currently the

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:27.750
she's the chair of a super PAC which supports
Senator Cruz, but she's working in a party

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:33.760
whose infighting, at least to me, makes me
think more of the Democrats than the traditional

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.760
Republicans.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:35.760
(Laughter.)

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.760
CONWAY: "Feel the Bern."

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.760
(Laughter.)

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:38.760
STEIN: Right.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.760
(Chuckles.)

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.820
Doug, in this season, has polled for a mayor
who finally decided not to run.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:48.370
And Scott has polled and done communications
strategy for a candidate who literally does

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.870
not exist as a human being.

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:57.600
So I'd like to focus our discussion on public
opinion and attitudes, and in our discussion

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.400
part not so much on rules, conventions, and
the like, and we can come to that with the

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.400
member questions.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.659
But let me begin with this.

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:12.260
Scott, would you open by telling us about
the "We are Smith" candidacy, how it came

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.269
to be and what you found and what your research
has shown?

00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:16.269
MILLER: Yeah.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:20.540
Three of us-Pat Caddell, who some of you may
know, the old Jimmy Carter wonderguy-actually,

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:27.590
the George McGovern wunderkind and still a
wunderkind as an aging wunderkind-Pat and

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:35.120
Bob Perkins, who's a longtime Republican warhorse,
and me, who's a recovering Democrat, got together.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.549
We were funded by Lee Hanley and Bob Mercer,
and have been doing research for about four

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:46.030
or five years-unbiased research not focused
on any particular candidate, but really sort

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:51.660
of plumbing the depths and the breadth of
this alienation, frustration, and anger that's

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.029
out there in the public.

00:02:54.029 --> 00:02:57.930
And we wanted-in trying to understand it,
one of the things we did to see if we could

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.939
channel it was to create a hypothetical candidate.

00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:02.559
That's candidate Smith.

00:03:02.559 --> 00:03:09.620
We said candidate Smith was-we never described
as man or woman, Democrat, Republican, independent.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:16.569
But Smith was someone who would come from
outside Washington-not a lawyer-(laughs)-not

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:22.150
a typical establishment Washingtonian, and
bring reform.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:27.549
But I-you know, and many people jokingly,
in the beginning, said, well, that's Trump

00:03:27.549 --> 00:03:29.519
or that's Bernie Sanders.

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:33.109
We specifically asked that question in our
last poll, and 81 percent of the people say

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:34.650
no.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.069
Smith is unique.

00:03:36.069 --> 00:03:41.909
But I want to-let me address just some of
the numbers about this level of anger and

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:42.909
alienation.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:44.069
People talk about angry white men.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:46.280
There are a lot of angry black women.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.340
There are a lot of angry Hispanic young people.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:55.969
Eighty-four percent of all voters of all demographics
in the United States believe that there's

00:03:55.969 --> 00:04:03.159
an elite group of incumbent politicians in
both parties, lobbyists, big banks, big business,

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:10.150
big unions, big special interests, big media
in Washington that rig the system for their

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.920
own power and prestige.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.689
How did they find out?

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:16.729
Eighty-four percent.

00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:17.859
That's an incredible level.

00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:21.340
I mean, it's at a revolutionary level.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.250
Seventy-eight percent of the people believe
that both parties are too beholden to special

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.110
interests to be able to do anything for the
country.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:32.280
And, of course, as you all know, I mean, about
two-thirds of the people believe that our

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.920
country's in decline.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.099
About two-thirds believe that our country
is not going to give a better nation to our

00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:39.970
kids than we've had for ourselves.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.362
So it's the abrogation of the American Dream.

00:04:42.362 --> 00:04:44.270
The anger is real.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:47.540
It's going to go way beyond this election.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:54.130
And we found that Smith, a reformer coming
to Washington and standing for sort of a common-sense,

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:59.800
boilerplate reform platform, could beat every
single candidate in the race, and still can,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:04.870
by 2-to-1-Clinton, Trump, Cruz, Sanders, and
on and on.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:10.080
STEIN: So, Doug, is this consistent with what
you found in the Bloomberg polling?

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.409
SCHOEN: Well, I was going to say, fortunately
or unfortunately, his name is Bloomberg, not

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:16.409
Smith.

00:05:16.409 --> 00:05:17.409
(Laughter.)

00:05:17.409 --> 00:05:27.650
So he didn't garner the level of support for
his own putative candidacy that Smith did

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:32.120
in the work that Scott and Bob and Pat did.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:38.069
But there is obviously-and your question points
it out, Skip-a commonality across it, because

00:05:38.069 --> 00:05:46.490
when you do broad levels of attitudinal polling
you find the level of anger that Scott speaks

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:52.650
of actually, in a certain sense, underestimates
what's going on.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:59.740
And there are really three discrete kinds
of anger, all of which I daresay-probably

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:06.780
not a great word, but-metastasize into a(n)
effective rejection of our politics and our

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:07.780
system.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:13.560
So there's anger on the right, which is: Donald
Trump, to hell with them, send a message,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:20.229
build the wall, let them know about corruption,
we want somebody independent, no ties.

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:21.500
So there's that kind.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:29.520
Then there's the kind of Ted Cruz, cultural,
political, anti-systemic, anti-Washington

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.150
conservatism that Kellyanne can speak about
much better than I can.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:40.520
What Scott was speaking of, and what Mayor
Bloomberg was seeking to assess in the work

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:48.230
we did, was the anger in the center-those
who don't vote in primaries who, nonetheless,

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:53.129
are looking for perhaps the opposite of what
those on the right and the left are looking

00:06:53.129 --> 00:07:01.249
at, which is nonpartisan conciliation, results-oriented
decision-making, a sort of a sense just enough

00:07:01.249 --> 00:07:05.940
of this crap, can we get down to the issues
and problems that we are facing.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:12.310
And, you know, one of the things I said this
week-which is not, Skip, a specific comment

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:17.860
about the election, but is representative
of what I think the problems we're facing

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:25.300
are-I did an interview, and somebody was asking
me about Trump versus protesters versus Lewandowski.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:30.259
And I said, look, we had a terror attack last
week in Brussels.

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:36.270
More people than were killed in Brussels were
killed in Lahore, Pakistan because they were

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.610
Christians celebrating Easter.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:46.669
Now, if we're talking about domestic political
violence in terms of fistfights and who's

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:51.409
grabbing whose arm, what does that say about
the campaign vis-�-vis the problems we're

00:07:51.409 --> 00:07:52.599
facing?

00:07:52.599 --> 00:07:58.120
For those on the left-and I should speak briefly
about it, because the Democratic race, while

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.819
I still believe it's Secretary Clinton's to
win, it has become much more competitive with

00:08:02.819 --> 00:08:10.250
Bernie Sanders' five straight victories-there's
the same level of anger, but it manifests

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:11.710
itself in a very different way.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:17.500
It's we need to redistribute wealth, we need
to break up the big banks, raise taxes on

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:23.740
the rich, the game is rigged-directed at young
people, because there are no guaranteed jobs,

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:29.330
you have this crushing student debt, and so
the American Dream isn't real for you.

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:35.169
That message is an authentic, clear, unambiguous
one that has, as you've seen particularly

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:38.570
to younger people and those on the left, had
broad resonance.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:45.830
But what is common across the board is everyone
is deeply, deeply enraged with a system that

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:52.440
is failing to address real problems and is
failing to produce any sort of a mechanism

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:56.850
to mediate or deal with those problems in
a way that gives any confidence.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:04.980
STEIN: So, Kellyanne, then, do you see the
theme of this election being establishment-overarching

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:12.670
theme-establishment versus anti-establishment,
and that being a more dominant concern than

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:20.210
any particular issues, that this is more of
an attitudinal and perspective situation?

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:25.400
CONWAY: Establishment versus non-establishment
is both a cause and a symptom in this election,

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:26.400
in my view.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:32.050
And let me back up a moment just on this entire
narrative of anger and rage.

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:37.330
I think that puts too much of the burden on
the voter, and it relieves the burden on those

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:41.230
who have created this great deal of frustration
and betrayal.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:48.070
"Betrayal" is a very important word in this
election cycle, because if pollsters would

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:52.630
supplant anger for the word "betrayal" or
"frustration," you find many voters left,

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:57.520
right, and center gravitating toward that
description of their own feeling toward the

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:03.500
political class, toward the establishment-again,
right, left, and center-than anger.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.200
And anger-Jeffrey Pollack is in the audience.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:11.380
We both worked for Frank Luntz in 1994, when
the Republican revolution happened.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.350
Anger was very, very pregnant in 1994.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:18.930
I think now people are struggling in a different
type of way.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:23.060
And their frustration at the system is that
they feel they don't have a voice, and they

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:27.570
feel nobody truly works for them, and that
it's a rigged game they cannot win.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:28.990
And, by the way, they have a point.

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:34.300
What we discovered about 14 months ago or
so in qualitative and quantitative research

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:39.420
I think has come true through this election
cycle, which is this: for all the anger and

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:45.190
ire directed at corporate America-the cronyism,
the corruption, the backroom dealing, the

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:50.210
lack of transparency, the haves versus the
have-nots-many voters now have transferred

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:54.630
that anger over to Washington and the political
class.

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:57.600
Doesn't mean that they've taken it away from
corporate America, but they're sharing the

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:58.600
burden now.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:03.710
And it's a very fascinating dynamic for folks
to actually think: precisely what I thought

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:07.180
about corporate America, I now think about
political America.

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:08.310
And we see that.

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:14.150
On the Republican side, it's been establishment
versus non-establishment as a pitched battle

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:15.780
almost from day one.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:21.620
And, yes, that is occupied predominantly by
Donald Trump, but don't forget it was Donald

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:25.340
Trump and Ben Carson who were the front-runners
for most of last summer.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:31.090
And then you throw in there Ted Cruz, the
original outsider-gratefully, with insider's

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:37.051
experience and seasoning, actually knows ways-knows
his way around Washington, doesn't have to

00:11:37.051 --> 00:11:43.220
navigate and think too hard about policy issues
because he's already thought about them-but

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:48.220
we no longer on the Republican side have the
debate between establishment versus non-establishment.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:52.070
The establishment in the Republican Party
is flat on its back.

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:56.590
All of its candidates are no longer in the
race, with the exception of John Kasich, who's

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:01.330
here in New York finding votes and not in
Wisconsin, where the battle is Tuesday.

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:06.130
So the argument now on the Republican side
is no longer establishment versus non-establishment.

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:09.360
It's now Trump versus non-Trump-not anti-Trump,
non-Trump.

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:14.900
And the big debate now is, how much of the
vote share and the growth opportunity within

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.300
those two vote shares exists?

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:23.760
So Trump has anywhere from 35 to 42 percent
of the vote share in most states right now

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:24.860
nationwide.

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:28.730
The non-Trump vote share is the remainder.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:32.380
And that's where the growth opportunity is,
because he usually does not get late-breaking

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:33.380
voters.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:38.550
On the Democratic side, it's very important
to the Republican Party to watch the Democratic

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:44.510
side also have its establishment versus non-establishment
battle, where you have a former first lady,

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:47.831
former United States senator, and, indeed,
former secretary of state getting 18 percent

00:12:47.831 --> 00:12:50.080
of the vote in Alaska this week.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.890
That is not the "vast right-wing conspiracy"
at work.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:13:00.770
That a Democratic primary and caucus situation
where the vast, vast majority of her own voters

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:06.840
are saying, hold on, we refuse to accede to
the establishment and be told-as Republican

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:13.490
voters have been told for years-who can win,
who can't win, who's electable, who's non-electable.

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:19.260
This is the year, ladies and gentlemen, when
Democratic and Republican voters rose up and

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:25.630
said: I will not be told how to think, who
to vote for, who can win, who can't win, who's

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:29.740
electable, who's not electable, who to coalesce
around 18 months before the general election.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:32.800
And you see that in these pitched battles
that still exist.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:35.930
MILLER: I think that's such a great point.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.260
And the point about the voters, too.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:42.890
I mean, the one thing I would hate to give
up on because of Trump is Trump's voters,

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:45.480
because they are not Trump.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:52.250
But one of the really interesting repudiations
of the establishment this year-you know, Jack

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:53.540
Leslie and Harris Diamond are here.

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:58.930
We were all working together in a political
company many, many years ago, and we in many

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:02.650
ways set some of the established ways of developing
a campaign.

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:10.520
There's probably been $300 million spent on
traditional negative advertising that has

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:12.870
always been just the bread and butter of campaigns.

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:14.440
Now they start with it.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:20.670
And it's the foreboding, dark voice; the ominous
tones; the black and white, unattractive photo

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:26.330
of the candidate, you know; the white type
comes across with accusations, and then some

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:33.320
snappy line; and, you know, People for America's
Rutabagas or whatever PAC-(laughter)-is sponsoring

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:34.320
it.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.510
Three hundred million dollars has not moved
the needle at all.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:39.150
SCHOEN: Talk to Jeb Bush.

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:44.830
MILLER: Yeah, $150 million that didn't do
anything-didn't gain-well, four delegates.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.340
CONWAY: Well, his consultants are building
new beach houses.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:48.340
SCHOEN: Yeah, exactly.

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:49.340
CONWAY: So there's that.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:51.930
SCHOEN: And, you know, one of the things Kellyanne
mentioned when we were talking the other day

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:56.630
was how they were-how you're actually deploying
resources in your super PAC is totally different.

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:58.530
STEIN: Yeah, talk about that for a minute.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:14:59.530
CONWAY: My pleasure.

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:05.240
So I run THE super PAC for Ted Cruz, and we've
been at this from the very beginning.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:10.990
That means that I cannot talk to candidate
Cruz or anybody, including volunteers to the

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:16.230
Cruz campaign, which means a number of elected
officials now we cannot speak with.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:21.100
And so the traditional model of the super
PAC-"traditional" being the last six years,

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:26.200
since the Citizens United decision came down-has
really been to collect check after check after

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.310
check, and run ad after ad after ad.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:33.710
And we tried to do things a little bit differently
this time, under some criticism at the very

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:36.150
beginning because people like to see ads.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:39.760
They like to just sit there, point and click,
and see who you're zinging today, and how

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:43.950
you're spending gobs and gobs of donors' money.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:48.670
And I told our primary donor from the very
beginning last spring, a year ago, thank you

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:54.410
for investing your resources; I am going to
subscribe to the old saw that a fastest way

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:59.400
to make a small fortune is to have a very
large one and waste most of it; and I will

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:02.710
be, you know, very deliberative about husbanding
these resources over time.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:03.710
So, what did we do?

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:05.350
We said, who is our candidate?

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:06.470
Our candidate is Ted Cruz.

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:07.790
Who is he?

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:10.370
He's the original bottom-up grassroots guy.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:12.150
He shows up at rallies.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:18.950
He shows up at these center-right confabs
where there are seven or 10 other speakers,

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:21.790
and nobody was getting the kind of reception
Ted Cruz was getting.

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:23.560
The grassroots literally are on their feet.

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:24.720
They love him.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:26.220
They're hooting and hollering.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:27.860
He serves up the red meat.

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:30.520
He's very policy-centric, issue-centric.

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:34.910
And he also is somebody who did not hold elective
office until he was in his 40s.

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:38.600
So there's so much about Ted Cruz that most
voters don't know.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:42.340
And there is so much that he was not willing
to fill in the blanks.

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:43.370
He is who he is.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:49.320
He's not going to talk about some of the raw
personal human connections that he has endured

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:51.640
in his life the way all of us have.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:56.120
He's not going to talk about his opponents
right way because he's actually not that kind

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:57.190
of candidate.

00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:00.810
So we developed a ground game, data analytics.

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:02.780
We invested in talk radio.

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:08.880
Our first buy was not TV ads, it was a million
dollars in talk radio-state-based talk radio

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:13.680
and national talk radio shows, which are much
less expensive and much more impactful if

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:19.380
your candidate is a guy who most Republican
primary and caucus voters got to know through

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:21.170
his leadership on Obamacare.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:28.690
So you have 54 Republican senators in Washington,
all of whom got there or stayed there in part

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:31.110
on their pledge to, quote, "repeal and replace"
Obamacare.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:36.300
But only one of them stood on his feet for
21 hours in the well of the U.S. Senate trying

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:38.870
to keep Obamacare from its final funding.

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:44.390
And so investing in that ground game, the
data analytics, and the really what we call

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:46.220
touch the voter at every level.

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:51.100
And we've run plenty of TV and cable ads,
but look at the super PACs that did almost

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:55.450
only that, and with 15, 20 percent commissions
to their consultants.

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:57.640
I call it staph infection in the Republican
Party.

00:17:57.640 --> 00:17:59.220
And I have a very happy, blessed life.

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:01.810
I've made a fortune as a Republican consultant.

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:04.620
And we're-and my guy's still standing, so
none of this is sour grapes.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:12.720
Make very, very clear that in 2012, the-President
Obama's consultants, particularly the pollsters,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:16.130
they were willing to give up part of their
budget-there's still plenty to go around,

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:22.230
folks-but to give it up to invest in a volunteer
program, a get-out-the-vote program, a voter-registration

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:24.010
program, a door-knocking program.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.770
This is very-these are very smart strategic
politics.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:31.530
So we just decided at the super PAC level
to try to support who our candidate was, and

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.580
we will continue to do that.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:38.540
I also think super PACs are-like pollsters,
are given too much credit for having too much

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:39.670
power.

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:42.140
I only wish that that were so, times two.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:47.310
The fact is, the super PACs have limits because
we can't coordinate the message with the candidate.

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:52.110
And I wake up every day trying not to embarrass
my candidate and try to support.

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:57.310
Now, when they do something, we either say
to ourselves, are they not running ads there

00:18:57.310 --> 00:19:02.070
because they want us to do it, or because
they don't think that they are viable in that

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:03.480
particular market?

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:09.870
So you're always-you know, you're always trying
to do your best and game the situation that

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:10.870
way.

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:17.620
STEIN: So, Doug, let me ask you, how did the
politicians and the parties and Congress and

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:23.900
the mainstream media so completely miss the
attitude in the country?

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:28.800
SCHOEN: Well, Skip, your question now-and
it's certainly one that Scott should answer

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:33.750
when I'm done, given the work he's done-is
really a fundamental one.

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:40.130
What has been so startling to me as somebody
who is, I guess, of the center, but certainly

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:46.990
looks closely at the left and the right, the
two areas where the degree of anger has been

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:53.410
most prominent-but what's been most surprising
to me is the degree to which the mainstream

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:59.480
media has totally missed with Kellyanne and
Scott and I are talking about this morning,

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:04.550
and which now I'm sure most of you would sort
of say, yeah, all of this makes sense.

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:10.550
But as it was playing out, it was very, very
clear in the poll data we collected the level

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:18.180
of anger, animus, particularly with Washington,
sense of corruption, failed government, failed

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:24.590
policies-a sense on the left that the game
was rigged and that we need broadly redistributive

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:29.440
policies if people are going to have a chance;
versus those on the right who want to, you

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:35.720
know, basically weaken further the power of
the federal government, certainly staunchly

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:36.800
anti-Obama.

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:43.100
But also, as Kellyanne might or might not
tell you, they are almost as angry towards

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:49.480
their congressional leadership on the Republican
side as they are towards President Obama and

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:50.880
the Democrats.

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:58.400
The Republican base feels so profoundly betrayed-the
word, I guess, Kellyanne and Scott were using

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:05.230
a couple of minutes ago-that they were willing
to vote for a Donald Trump, who certainly

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:10.660
for a long time was mouthing the concerns,
the frustrations, and the anger that the base

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:12.600
has felt.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:19.370
So there has been a radical disconnect in
this country between mass attitudes and what's

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:26.680
happened at the elite level to the point where
I think many of us almost look askance on

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:31.300
a daily basis at the newspaper headlines.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:36.450
And I guess this morning, when I woke up and
I looked at a headline in the Times that said

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:42.920
"Trump Finally Slipping," I sort of was waiting
to read the "ha, ha, ha, we told you so."

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:47.960
And there are plenty of reasons Donald Trump
is slipping, but it would be a mistake to

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:51.850
believe in that in some way the establishment
is asserting itself.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:56.520
If it is, to assert itself through Ted Cruz
is a really pretty strange way to do it.

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:57.520
(Laughter.)

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:02.680
But on the-but on the Democratic side-and
I don't want to give short shrift-Secretary

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:06.980
Clinton's ratings are-her negative is close
to 58, 59 (percent).

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:11.100
I had a piece in the Journal to that effect
a couple of weeks ago.

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:15.390
Her lack of trust rating is over 60 percent.

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:21.200
Bernie Sanders, as I said, five straight,
within 12 points in the Quinnipiac poll in

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:27.470
New York state that just, I think, came out
the other day, before the New York campaign

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:28.470
has fully engaged.

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:34.630
I suspect Senator Sanders, breaking Skip's
edict, will probably win Wisconsin at the

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:37.320
same time that Ted Cruz does.

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:48.090
But this speaks boldly and directly to a disjunction
between the mainstream media and the voters,

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:53.380
because if I had sat here five months ago,
or maybe even three months ago, and say we'd

00:22:53.380 --> 00:23:01.390
see Bernie Sanders surging, Ted Cruz surging
as the candidate of the establishment, I think

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:05.710
most of you would have said it'll be a long
time before we invite Schoen back.

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:06.710
(Laughter.)

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:11.430
MILLER: You know, I think-Caddell says, well,
if you have to make a bet, bet on chaos at

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:12.430
this time.

00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:18.120
But I'm astounded, as we looked at this research
for-over the last four or five years, that,

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:23.140
as Doug says, that the media never got it,
still doesn't get it, and that none of the

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:24.140
candidates got it.

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:28.701
I mean, most of them thought that this would
be a traditional ideological campaign, conservative

00:23:28.701 --> 00:23:30.280
versus Democrat.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:35.170
Even with the-versus liberal, conservative-liberal,
even conservative or real conservative.

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:37.500
And many have argued that and still do.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:41.860
Some think it's still an issue campaign, sort
of a women's reproductive rights versus gun

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:44.390
rights, or whatever the issue of the day might
be.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:45.630
It is not.

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:47.280
This is an insurgency.

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:50.690
This is the people versus the establishment,
the people versus the government.

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:55.870
If this were a democratic election in a Third
World, it would be the people, you know, versus

00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:59.640
the entrenched, corrupt incumbent.

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:03.610
And they're going to keep trying.

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:05.070
And they keep trying.

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:12.030
I mean, again-I'm not a-I'm sorry, Kellyanne,
I'm not a Ted Cruz fan, and I kind of-I loved

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:17.270
what Peggy Noonan said this morning, which
is only Donald Trump could make Ted Cruz seem

00:24:17.270 --> 00:24:18.270
normal.

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:19.270
(Laughter, laughs.)

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:24.840
But it's true, but I mean, and not to be pejorative-
CONWAY: Well, you just-(laughter)-well, you

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:25.840
just were.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.870
MILLER: No, but I-
SCHOEN: But when will you be pejorative?

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:29.870
(Laughter.)

00:24:29.870 --> 00:24:34.770
MILLER: But he-but the guy is-but, I mean,
you know, he's a guy who was very, very controversial,

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:38.870
and now he is the establishment hope and may
very well be the Republican candidate.

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:41.160
CONWAY: I'm sorry, I don't know what made
him so controversial.

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:44.679
Was it the fact that he stood up to leaders
of both parties, which is exactly-

00:24:44.679 --> 00:24:48.300
SCHOEN: Well, shutting down the government
is usually not a tactic that freshman senators

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:49.300
take.

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:50.300
CONWAY: No, he didn't shut down the government.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:51.300
Obama shut down the government.

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:52.760
All he did was stand on his feet to try to
keep-

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:53.760
SCHOEN: Exactly my point.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:55.059
CONWAY: -an entitlement from taking hold.

00:24:55.059 --> 00:24:59.400
The fact is, here's Ted Cruz, has a good shot
of being the Republican nominee and everybody's

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:00.980
going to have to learn to deal with it.

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:05.380
I would also just point out-(laughter)-I would
also just point out-including Mrs. Clinton,

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:06.399
by the way.

00:25:06.399 --> 00:25:12.940
I would also point out that every time someone
has a-every time publicly and of note somebody

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:16.249
has attacked Senator Cruz this cycle, how
has he responded?

00:25:16.249 --> 00:25:17.249
In kind?

00:25:17.249 --> 00:25:18.710
No, very kindly.

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:24.700
Former President George W. Bush, whose brother
has now endorsed Ted-two brothers have endorsed

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:30.790
Ted Cruz for president, Neil and Jeb, and
whose brother ran a terrible campaign this

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:33.390
past time, he said, about Ted Cruz, "I just
don't like that guy."

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:35.000
What did Ted Cruz say in response?

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:38.880
"You were an awful president whose approval
rating was 28 percent when you left office"?

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:43.940
No, he said, I'll always be grateful that
Jeb-that George W. Bush was the president

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:47.460
of the United States on 9/11 and that I met
my wife, Heidi, during his campaign.

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:51.390
He's incredibly gracious, and people will
see that when it comes down to he versus Hillary

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:52.390
Clinton.

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:55.940
They certainly are seeing it when it's he
versus Donald Trump.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:58.940
So we've heard-in Cruz World, we've heard
it all before.

00:25:58.940 --> 00:25:59.940
(Chuckles.)

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.420
So-
STEIN: OK, so two more quick questions before

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:04.060
we go to members.

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:14.710
One is, Scott, if this-if the election was
purely based on just a public vote with no

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:17.370
party system at all, how would the-how would
the Smith candidate or a center candidate

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:18.370
have done?

00:26:18.370 --> 00:26:20.450
If there was no party system to worry about.

00:26:20.450 --> 00:26:27.540
MILLER: A center candidate who was from outside
Washington, and who opposed the status quo

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:33.210
and had a platform of real reform, would win,
I mean, under any circumstance.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:36.420
CONWAY: Yeah, well, that's like the blind
date you haven't met yet.

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:37.420
MILLER: Yeah, exactly.

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:38.420
CONWAY: I mean, that's-
STEIN: And I understand.

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:39.420
But-
CONWAY: I mean, eventually you have to open

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:41.340
the door and-(laughter)-
STEIN: No, I understand.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:46.050
But it-but it's a question-but it's a question
of attitudes.

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:51.750
SCHOEN: But Kellyanne's question actually,
or point, raises the real problem, which is

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:54.470
why there is so much anger.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:27:01.180
In modern-day America, the way the parties
have rigged the game, you can't get to the

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:02.540
door to open it.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:09.240
The political Tinder that Kellyanne is speaking
of-that's a joke, folks, if you-ask your teenagers

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:10.240
about Tinder.

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:11.240
(Laughter.)

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:17.250
And it doesn't exist because Smith isn't on
the ballot, Bloomberg's not on the ballot.

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:18.920
There are no independents.

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:24.510
There really has not been a viable independent
since 1992, and there almost certainly won't

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:25.790
be this year.

00:27:25.790 --> 00:27:31.640
But it really means that this is one of the
few so-called democracies where anti-systemic

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:38.590
politics just can't get a foothold unless
you happen to be a Donald Trump or a Bernie

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:42.500
Sanders-like figure with arguable unique appeals.

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:44.690
CONWAY: And I just want to add to that.

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:50.420
I think, but for Donald Trump, the biggest
story of this cycle by far is Bernie Sanders.

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:54.940
And it doesn't get enough-he doesn't get enough
coverage because he has no super PAC, he gets

00:27:54.940 --> 00:28:02.100
on TV and he says donate right now, BernieSanders.com
or whatnot, and he raised $44 million in March.

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:07.230
He raised $43 million in February, in small
donations.

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:12.910
That's incredibly courageous of people all
across the country to go against their parties-he's

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:17.770
not a third-party candidate, although he seems
to be-to go against their own party's leader

00:28:17.770 --> 00:28:19.730
and the next in line.

00:28:19.730 --> 00:28:24.310
It's actually been a role reversal for the
two political parties this time in this way.

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:29.920
It's my Republican Party that has suffered-and
I use the word advisedly-suffered from royalism:

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:32.770
let's nominate the person who lost to the
person who won.

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:33.880
That's really smart.

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:35.429
That should work this time.

00:28:35.429 --> 00:28:36.720
And it never does.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:43.380
And it's the Democratic Party that's been
masterful at elevating, and indeed electing,

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:49.671
figures-male figures-that represent transformational
generational change-JFK, Barack Obama, Bill

00:28:49.671 --> 00:28:50.671
Clinton.

00:28:50.671 --> 00:28:54.910
Even Jimmy Carter was 52 years old with a
9-year-old daughter when he got to the White

00:28:54.910 --> 00:28:55.910
House.

00:28:55.910 --> 00:28:56.910
That's young.

00:28:56.910 --> 00:29:00.610
And we-I love these guys, with all due respect
to them as public servants-thank you for your

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:04.430
service-but we get John McCain, Bob Dole,
and Mitt Romney.

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:09.480
And so this is the time-(laughter)-this was
the time that the roles actually got reversed.

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:16.080
We had a couple of 40-something sons of Cuban
American patriots.

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:19.310
We had governors in their 40s and 50s running.

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:24.880
And we have Donald Trump and Bernie Sanders,
close to 70 and 75 respectively, representing

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:25.880
transformational change.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:31.850
But the story of Bernie Sanders is the one
that, in my view, has proven most when people

00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:35.880
want to rise up and take over the system,
they can.

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:39.210
And they will not be told by the elites what
to think.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:40.770
Record turnout at rallies.

00:29:40.770 --> 00:29:42.070
Record turnout at the polls.

00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:44.200
It is really remarkable.

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:46.130
Let's give credit to the voters this time.

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:48.429
I don't really like calling them angry.

00:29:48.429 --> 00:29:52.270
The fact that you would stand in line for
two, three hours to cast a vote is pretty

00:29:52.270 --> 00:29:53.390
remarkable.

00:29:53.390 --> 00:29:55.670
It actually gives me a little bit of faith
in democracy.

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:58.929
It means that people still think their voice
and their vote counts.

00:29:58.929 --> 00:30:03.809
I mean, these are people who won't even come
out of their house for two weeks at a time,

00:30:03.809 --> 00:30:08.500
staring at screens all day, and having food
delivered, and not making eye contact with

00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:09.500
other people.

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:11.900
They showed up to vote in these primaries
and caucuses.

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:14.350
That's a big message for all of us.

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:18.960
STEIN: We could go on for a lot longer, but
let me turn it over to the members for questions.

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:22.830
So I'd remind everybody, this session is on
the record.

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:25.320
And please wait until the microphone comes
to you.

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:33.310
And if you could, keep your question short
so we can take as many questions as possible.

00:30:33.310 --> 00:30:35.210
Rita.

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:38.679
Q: Rita Hauser.

00:30:38.679 --> 00:30:40.950
You just touched briefly on Citizens United.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:43.270
And I'd like to come back to that.

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:48.590
I, as a lawyer, personally believe it will
go down as one of the worst decisions of the

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:51.370
court, perhaps since Dred Scott.

00:30:51.370 --> 00:30:54.929
What do we see, ordinary people as well as
we the elites?

00:30:54.929 --> 00:31:03.536
You see all the candidates making pilgrimages
to the Kochs, and to the Adelsons, and to

00:31:03.536 --> 00:31:04.536
I don't know who on the Democratic side.

00:31:04.536 --> 00:31:05.600
And everybody in the country sees that.

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:06.600
CONWAY: George Soros, Tom Steyer.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:07.600
Q: George Soros, whoever they all are.

00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:08.600
CONWAY: Shall I go on?

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:10.520
Q: But everybody in the country sees this.

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:16.470
They see these rich guys calling all the candidates
together, as someone said, kissing the ring.

00:31:16.470 --> 00:31:17.730
And they must stand back.

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:24.130
For the first time in my life as a very active
political person I have not contributed one

00:31:24.130 --> 00:31:31.950
cent to anybody or anything, so alienated
am I by this process of going out to meet

00:31:31.950 --> 00:31:35.490
the big guys who tell you what to do and where
the money comes from.

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:39.890
And I think that that is a very crucial part
of the anger that you see.

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:44.390
So it's miraculous that Sanders is getting
10 bucks and 50 bucks and so on.

00:31:44.390 --> 00:31:46.929
CONWAY: It also tells you that the super PACs
don't have as much power to appoint somebody

00:31:46.929 --> 00:31:50.530
who runs-
Q: They may not, but they have had a nefarious

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:53.500
picture and impact that people feel.

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:58.390
And I do think that that's really important,
because what you've all talked about skips

00:31:58.390 --> 00:32:03.540
around the question of how the hell do we
reform this system going forward, whatever

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:04.540
happens in this election.

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:07.980
MILLER: Well, one of the things we do is the
voters broke it this year, right?

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:08.980
Q: Yes.

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:09.980
MILLER: Right?

00:32:09.980 --> 00:32:18.420
Trump and-they've pulled Trump and Sanders
up, and they broke the system.

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:25.990
We've redistributed wealth to, you know, local
TV stations-(laughter)-from these super PACs

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:29.010
that were supporting Jeb Bush and others.

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:34.620
So in a sense, the American people are changing
that system by making it less effective.

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:36.920
But you're right, it's completely corrupt.

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:42.090
I mean, it's totally-how more corrupt could
you be than if you go to the right person

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:45.980
and say the right thing, and tell them you'll
do the right thing, that they'll give you

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:47.910
money in an endless amount.

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:49.990
CONWAY: But you presume that's the way it
works.

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:51.490
How do you know who's meeting whom?

00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:52.900
You presume that's the way it works.

00:32:52.900 --> 00:32:58.269
The last Koch summit was televised on TV,
the five candidates who were there in August.

00:32:58.269 --> 00:32:59.290
And that was on TV.

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:03.760
If you missed it, then that's on you, not
on them.

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:09.950
The five candidate conversations were broadcast
on, I believe it was C-SPAN, but don't quote

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:10.950
me.

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:11.950
It was somewhere.

00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:12.950
And you can go find it.

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:14.130
It was certainly livestreamed.

00:33:14.130 --> 00:33:18.630
But I think corruption is a very strong word
when you're talking about how the two insurgent

00:33:18.630 --> 00:33:22.480
candidates this time, Trump and Sanders, have
no super PACs.

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:26.320
And they make very clear that that's part
of their affirmative message when they speak.

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:31.750
If President Obama, who's very fond of the
pen and pad, very fond of the executive order,

00:33:31.750 --> 00:33:35.550
really didn't like super PACs I suppose he
would have passed an executive order by now.

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:38.100
But he had super PACs in 2012.

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:42.419
He railed against them, and then he enjoyed
their support.

00:33:42.419 --> 00:33:49.679
I will tell you this about money in politics:
If you don't like it, reform it.

00:33:49.679 --> 00:33:51.480
That's been tried.

00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:57.190
That's been-I have to say that the idea that
the super PACs are only benefitting people

00:33:57.190 --> 00:33:59.130
on the Republican side is crazy.

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:05.820
And the idea that Hillary Clinton, a former
first lady, a former United States senator,

00:34:05.820 --> 00:34:10.700
the immediate past secretary of state, doesn't
have money advantages over everybody else

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:13.099
in the race is just a ridiculous proposition.

00:34:13.099 --> 00:34:14.470
Of course she does.

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:16.989
So people are always looking for ways to level
the playing field.

00:34:16.989 --> 00:34:23.250
I can tell you that with one exception I receive
no super PAC donation that comes close to

00:34:23.250 --> 00:34:27.839
what Mrs. Clinton gets for a 25-minute speech
down the street here at Wall Street.

00:34:27.839 --> 00:34:30.129
So there's all types of money in politics
that people don't like.

00:34:30.129 --> 00:34:34.490
And I think but for those speeches she would
have an easier time being the Democratic nominee.

00:34:34.490 --> 00:34:36.210
So you can't just talk about super PACs.

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:41.319
You have to talk about all the money grabbing
among politicians that is bothering people

00:34:41.319 --> 00:34:42.319
this cycle.

00:34:42.319 --> 00:34:48.639
MILLER: But you got to remember, in 2015 Bernie
Sanders made $1,850 making speeches.

00:34:48.639 --> 00:34:50.010
And he gave it all to-
CONWAY: And there he is.

00:34:50.010 --> 00:34:51.010
I hear you.

00:34:51.010 --> 00:34:52.010
That's my point.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:53.010
MILLER: No, he gave it all to charity.

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:55.599
He made $1,400 the year before, gave it all
to charity.

00:34:55.599 --> 00:35:01.279
In that same time period, another candidate
in his party made $21 million making speeches.

00:35:01.279 --> 00:35:02.319
CONWAY: Bingo.

00:35:02.319 --> 00:35:09.069
SCHOEN: I would urge you to read Jane Mayer's
book on dark money, because it's not a simple

00:35:09.069 --> 00:35:12.059
case of give and get.

00:35:12.059 --> 00:35:21.109
It's more about how the nature of politics
has changed to an interlocking group of super

00:35:21.109 --> 00:35:28.559
PACs, lobbyists, special interests, particularly
in off-year elections, influencing the process.

00:35:28.559 --> 00:35:32.400
And commenting on-this isn't a comment about
the left versus the right.

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:37.979
I understand completely that there are good
guys and bad guys in your terms.

00:35:37.979 --> 00:35:42.339
And to my point, they're all bad guys and
it's a bad system.

00:35:42.339 --> 00:35:50.119
And whether the presidential election is or
is not impacted by a super PAC or PACs is,

00:35:50.119 --> 00:35:52.400
to me, not really the question.

00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:57.430
The question is does this bring us closer
to representative democracy?

00:35:57.430 --> 00:36:01.849
Does it strengthen the bond between the citizenry
and the elected officials?

00:36:01.849 --> 00:36:11.690
And I think if you read Mayer's book, which
is quite rightly tilted against the right,

00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:16.470
but I don't think it should be written as
a left-wing screed against the right.

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:24.319
I think you need to see it as a system of
politics that is very different than the one

00:36:24.319 --> 00:36:29.470
that Rita Hauser was describing and I grew
up with, that has emerged.

00:36:29.470 --> 00:36:31.109
It doesn't have to be corrupt.

00:36:31.109 --> 00:36:33.240
It doesn't even have to be efficacious.

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:39.180
It just is one that I think perverts democracy
as I've come to believe it should be practiced.

00:36:39.180 --> 00:36:41.210
STEIN: The gentleman-
Q: Hi.

00:36:41.210 --> 00:36:44.440
I'm Martin Gross from Sandalwood.

00:36:44.440 --> 00:36:47.700
If you-I guess if you want to get corporate
money out of politics that's OK, if you get

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:49.099
union money out of politics.

00:36:49.099 --> 00:36:52.980
If you get it all out, but let's just not
let us pick one side.

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:57.099
You guys have described something very interesting,
and that is that the pollsters didn't get

00:36:57.099 --> 00:37:00.839
the anger, and therefore a lot of what they
thought wouldn't happen did.

00:37:00.839 --> 00:37:05.150
So let's got to the next possible miscalculation.

00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:12.589
Go to the election, OK, and let's say it's
Clinton-Cruz, Clinton-Trump, OK?

00:37:12.589 --> 00:37:18.640
Either of-each of those candidates has very
significant negatives, as you've pointed out.

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:26.239
So how do you think that the American public
will react in terms of turnout, in terms of

00:37:26.239 --> 00:37:33.160
how you think it will behave if it's presented
with a choice that a very significant number

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:38.579
of Americans find neither of the candidates
very palatable at all.

00:37:38.579 --> 00:37:43.029
Can you see significantly much less turnout
than you would have thought?

00:37:43.029 --> 00:37:48.430
How do you think that'll play out, given what
you've described of this asymmetry, so to

00:37:48.430 --> 00:37:53.029
speak, between what Americans are thinking
and feeling, and what the choice is they're

00:37:53.029 --> 00:37:54.400
being presented with?

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:59.500
CONWAY: To me, the big question lies in what
Chris Matthews referred to last night on the

00:37:59.500 --> 00:38:03.239
show we appeared on together, the Charlie
Rose show, as a marriage of convenience for

00:38:03.239 --> 00:38:04.239
Hillary Clinton.

00:38:04.239 --> 00:38:08.130
And I recoiled, and he said: The marriage
of convenience between Hillary Clinton and

00:38:08.130 --> 00:38:09.690
Barack Obama.

00:38:09.690 --> 00:38:11.470
And I think that's a very important point.

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:17.240
If the President goes out and campaigns side-by-side
with Hillary Clinton in all of October, it's

00:38:17.240 --> 00:38:22.950
huge for her, not just because it brings out
the minority vote, which has been incredibly

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:24.369
important to her in these primaries.

00:38:24.369 --> 00:38:30.549
I mean, she's-men don't like her, don't want
her, don't respect her, don't-dismiss her

00:38:30.549 --> 00:38:35.510
commander in chief and secretary-and president,
by and large.

00:38:35.510 --> 00:38:39.920
She's got about 35, 37 percent of the white
male vote right now.

00:38:39.920 --> 00:38:42.489
But with his-I think it's important for another
reason.

00:38:42.489 --> 00:38:47.380
With the president's imprimatur next to her
basically saying she's going to start year

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:54.400
nine of the Obama presidency right now, vote
for her, that's a wild card we're not even-I

00:38:54.400 --> 00:38:58.990
think we, as a political culture, aren't examining
deeply enough yet.

00:38:58.990 --> 00:39:00.650
So that will matter.

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:06.720
Her negatives could stay as high as they are
and she'll still get elected.

00:39:06.720 --> 00:39:10.099
Donald Trump's negatives are different because
they're more recent.

00:39:10.099 --> 00:39:11.999
Hers have been that way for a while now.

00:39:11.999 --> 00:39:16.980
She earned them quite a while ago and she's
done nothing to unearn them and improve them.

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.980
And she hasn't worn well on this campaign
trail, there's no doubt.

00:39:20.980 --> 00:39:22.230
But Donald Trump's are more recent.

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:26.710
He's had the worst 72 hours, I think, in his
campaign, or the worst week in his campaign

00:39:26.710 --> 00:39:27.849
to date.

00:39:27.849 --> 00:39:35.029
And his negatives among women are so high
now that it's really a shame, because Hillary

00:39:35.029 --> 00:39:39.079
Clinton's negatives among men as such that
we could have a conversation about the gender

00:39:39.079 --> 00:39:43.720
gap a year from now that is not about the
Republican Party's problem engaging female

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:48.999
voters, it's Hillary's problem in engaging
a sufficient number of male voters-particularly

00:39:48.999 --> 00:39:51.549
white male voters, in the 2016 election.

00:39:51.549 --> 00:39:56.869
All that washes away if you nominate Donald
Trump and if his negatives among women stay

00:39:56.869 --> 00:39:58.210
so high.

00:39:58.210 --> 00:40:03.410
I think turnout if you've got a Sanders or
a Trump will be very high, probably be high

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:07.900
if you have a Cruz because he's seen as an
anti-establishment outsider.

00:40:07.900 --> 00:40:11.959
But I think President Obama helping Secretary
Clinton is huge-huge.

00:40:11.959 --> 00:40:18.500
And on the conservatives, on the Republicans'
side, let me say this: The Republicans should

00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:22.739
have seen the anger, frustration, and betrayal
because that's how they won the elections

00:40:22.739 --> 00:40:24.109
in 2010 and 2014.

00:40:24.109 --> 00:40:28.739
It's as if we wake up and it's a presidential
year and they say, oh, none of that matters.

00:40:28.739 --> 00:40:30.489
It's not a different set of voters.

00:40:30.489 --> 00:40:32.780
It shouldn't be a different set of circumstances.

00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:37.540
It ultimately will be a large amount of money
spent a different opponent-Hillary Clinton

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:40.329
or Bernie Sanders-but it's still the same
voters.

00:40:40.329 --> 00:40:45.789
And it's the voters who came out and helped
the Republicans in 2010, in 2014, when most

00:40:45.789 --> 00:40:47.930
things got nailed to the ground.

00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:51.839
And they came-those voters came back this
time and said: We are not going backwards

00:40:51.839 --> 00:40:54.029
into one of those mushy, moderate candidates.

00:40:54.029 --> 00:40:55.670
So they should have seen that coming.

00:40:55.670 --> 00:41:00.589
I think you're going to see record turnout
this time, but I think if it's Clinton-I always

00:41:00.589 --> 00:41:05.359
thought if it was, like, Hillary Clinton versus
Jeb Bush you wouldn't because people who say,

00:41:05.359 --> 00:41:06.359
who cares?

00:41:06.359 --> 00:41:11.329
And I actually thought first woman versus
third Bush would never have been a fair fight.

00:41:11.329 --> 00:41:16.219
So that's a relief.

00:41:16.219 --> 00:41:18.660
STEIN: Ma'am.

00:41:18.660 --> 00:41:23.150
Q: George de Menil, Paris School of Economics.

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:34.180
I feel as if I were in a political meeting
of the Jacobin club in 1788, where we're all

00:41:34.180 --> 00:41:39.780
inviting, encouraging revolution.

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:51.109
And my question, though, is: What do you think
of a different proposition, that the media

00:41:51.109 --> 00:41:57.839
and the growing importance of the media are
empowering populism?

00:41:57.839 --> 00:42:02.440
The media didn't see Trump coming, didn't
see a number of other things, didn't see the

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:06.510
anger, but they've certainly played a big
role in it.

00:42:06.510 --> 00:42:11.920
And I think that this is a phenomenon we're
going to continue to have to deal with.

00:42:11.920 --> 00:42:19.670
MILLER: Well, I think-you know, again, my
friends out there, my old pals, we've watched

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:22.999
the American people vote for change every
time they got a chance.

00:42:22.999 --> 00:42:30.430
Every single election I can remember in my
adult life has been change, you know, and

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:31.430
hope essentially.

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:34.640
And they've been thwarted every time.

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:37.809
And this is a revolution.

00:42:37.809 --> 00:42:38.950
The people now see.

00:42:38.950 --> 00:42:42.849
They've got the bit in their teeth and they're
running.

00:42:42.849 --> 00:42:44.999
And I think they deserve to.

00:42:44.999 --> 00:42:49.999
I'm with Doug on the state of politics today.

00:42:49.999 --> 00:42:52.849
Politics earned this revolution.

00:42:52.849 --> 00:42:54.569
And I hope it's sweeping.

00:42:54.569 --> 00:42:55.579
I hope it really is sweeping.

00:42:55.579 --> 00:42:58.660
And I hope it extends far beyond 2016.

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:00.989
STEIN: Ma'am.

00:43:00.989 --> 00:43:04.489
Q: Bettye Musham.

00:43:04.489 --> 00:43:08.960
The media keeps saying that women are going
to elect the next president.

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:15.710
But I think the number of women voting has
gone down since we were allowed to vote.

00:43:15.710 --> 00:43:19.559
So who are these new women that are going
to elect the new president?

00:43:19.559 --> 00:43:23.619
Are they new people coming into the system?

00:43:23.619 --> 00:43:24.619
Who are they?

00:43:24.619 --> 00:43:26.890
STEIN: Kellyanne, why don't you-
CONWAY: Thank you.

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:28.279
No, that's actually not true.

00:43:28.279 --> 00:43:32.289
Women have comprised a majority of the presidential
electorate since 1964.

00:43:32.289 --> 00:43:36.589
And that's been true for 47, 50 years now.

00:43:36.589 --> 00:43:40.924
Q: Not the number of women that are registered
to vote.

00:43:40.924 --> 00:43:41.924
That's going down-
CONWAY: Well, that may be true.

00:43:41.924 --> 00:43:44.470
The number of women registered to vote is
true.

00:43:44.470 --> 00:43:48.760
And in part, because of something that everybody
has touched on today, and particularly in

00:43:48.760 --> 00:43:52.619
Doug's work for Mayor Bloomberg, which is
people feel disaffected from the system and

00:43:52.619 --> 00:43:54.349
they say: Why even bother?

00:43:54.349 --> 00:43:55.530
It's not my fault.

00:43:55.530 --> 00:43:57.000
It's the system's fault.

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:59.660
Don't call me the lazy, apathetic, disengaged
voter.

00:43:59.660 --> 00:44:02.339
I haven't seen any reason to feel engaged
or inspired.

00:44:02.339 --> 00:44:05.079
MILLER: It's astounding that 30 percent of
the people do turn out.

00:44:05.079 --> 00:44:06.510
CONWAY: Well, yeah, that's right.

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:07.950
That's right, given the way they feel.

00:44:07.950 --> 00:44:12.869
But on women, I suspect they will continue
to be a majority of the electorate.

00:44:12.869 --> 00:44:19.710
The genders actually represent their percentage
of the population at the ballot box, unlike

00:44:19.710 --> 00:44:23.130
my age or by race, traditionally, or even
by geography.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:27.999
The gender is pretty much 52 percent/48 (percent),
53/47 (percent).

00:44:27.999 --> 00:44:33.859
So women have elected the presidents, so to
speak, in the last 50 years.

00:44:33.859 --> 00:44:35.900
The question is, what motivates them?

00:44:35.900 --> 00:44:39.930
Are they really going to go and vote on abortion
and contraception?

00:44:39.930 --> 00:44:44.020
Are they going-and most women fill up the
gas tank and the grocery cart each week, they

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:45.580
don't get an abortion.

00:44:45.580 --> 00:44:50.210
You can care about that issue, have an opinion
on it, but it's not a big vote motivator.

00:44:50.210 --> 00:44:51.380
It's not in the top five.

00:44:51.380 --> 00:44:55.369
And particularly when national security and
terrorism has popped up.

00:44:55.369 --> 00:45:00.880
And on social issues like that, that's already-everybody
already knows where they are with that.

00:45:00.880 --> 00:45:03.300
The goalposts tend to move on the economy.

00:45:03.300 --> 00:45:08.220
They tend to move on national security and
terrorism when different events happen.

00:45:08.220 --> 00:45:12.660
And I would just say that women particularly
have led the charge.

00:45:12.660 --> 00:45:20.029
Since 1960 we, as an electorate, but particularly
among female voters, have gone-with the exception

00:45:20.029 --> 00:45:26.421
of 1972-we've gone for the presidential candidate
that we have seen as more optimistic, more

00:45:26.421 --> 00:45:30.719
forward-looking, frankly just more pleasant,
if not visionary.

00:45:30.719 --> 00:45:33.869
So you've got Barack Obama beating John McCain
and Mitt Romney.

00:45:33.869 --> 00:45:37.979
You have George W. Bush beating Al Gore and
John Kerry.

00:45:37.979 --> 00:45:40.289
You have JFK beating Dick Nixon.

00:45:40.289 --> 00:45:45.549
But anyway, we prefer that overall, in addition
to the issues that we're focused on.

00:45:45.549 --> 00:45:52.160
I look at the split screen between Sanders
and Clinton and I'm astonished that Bernie

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:53.160
Sanders has it right.

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:54.349
What do all of his signs say?

00:45:54.349 --> 00:45:56.910
"A Future We Can Believe In."

00:45:56.910 --> 00:45:59.670
That's the kind of-see, you're nodding your
head now, "Future We Can Believe In."

00:45:59.670 --> 00:46:03.460
Hillary Clinton's signs say, "Fighting for
Us."

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:08.369
That's unusual, just given the way female
voters particularly end up making-(inaudible).

00:46:08.369 --> 00:46:13.640
But let me make something last very clear,
Hillary Clinton is having a hard time with

00:46:13.640 --> 00:46:15.569
women in her own party.

00:46:15.569 --> 00:46:19.900
She lost them to Barack Obama in 2008, and
he became the nominee.

00:46:19.900 --> 00:46:24.489
When you had 56 percent of the Democratic
voters in 2008 were women.

00:46:24.489 --> 00:46:28.150
So this nonsense that the vast, right-wing
conspiracy stands in her way, we never even

00:46:28.150 --> 00:46:29.150
got a shot at her.

00:46:29.150 --> 00:46:30.960
We don't vote in the Democratic primaries.

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:31.960
(Laughter.)

00:46:31.960 --> 00:46:33.510
It was the people-women in her own party who
voted the other way.

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:37.060
MILLER: Kellyanne, she's now more worried
about a vast, left-wing conspiracy.

00:46:37.060 --> 00:46:38.060
(Laughter.)

00:46:38.060 --> 00:46:39.060
CONWAY: That's right.

00:46:39.060 --> 00:46:40.060
There you go.

00:46:40.060 --> 00:46:41.170
But women will be there.

00:46:41.170 --> 00:46:44.880
STEIN: The gentleman-yes, right.

00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:50.710
Q: My name is Larry Bridwell and I teach at
Pace University.

00:46:50.710 --> 00:46:55.920
There's been this emphasis on "Make America
Great Again" by Donald Trump.

00:46:55.920 --> 00:47:00.349
And then there's Hillary saying America never
stopped being great.

00:47:00.349 --> 00:47:08.140
How is this idea of the United States being
or not being a great country playing out this

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:09.140
year?

00:47:09.140 --> 00:47:10.140
SCHOEN: Sure.

00:47:10.140 --> 00:47:18.190
Well, the comment is actually-I think gets
at a complicated dimension of our position

00:47:18.190 --> 00:47:19.390
globally.

00:47:19.390 --> 00:47:24.910
Undeniably and particularly on the right,
there is a sense that our influence has waned

00:47:24.910 --> 00:47:31.809
around the world, and that our stature around
the world-whether it be economically, socially,

00:47:31.809 --> 00:47:37.839
certainly politically given John Kerry's comments
of a count of days ago, but ultimately militarily.

00:47:37.839 --> 00:47:45.309
And on the right, it's we really are, you
know, a much weaker country.

00:47:45.309 --> 00:47:51.740
The flipside, which has gotten less attention,
is for a lot of people in the center and particularly

00:47:51.740 --> 00:47:57.869
on the left there is still a sense that this
is a great nation, but it is a great nation

00:47:57.869 --> 00:48:05.059
not delivering on its promises to the citizenry,
hence the Bernie Sanders campaign.

00:48:05.059 --> 00:48:13.010
And indeed, what I think is in-artful about
the dialogue that the candidates have had,

00:48:13.010 --> 00:48:18.519
is Hillary Clinton really hasn't had-been
able to figure out how to respond.

00:48:18.519 --> 00:48:24.660
Because when she says "Make American Whole
Again," it's pretty hard to figure out what

00:48:24.660 --> 00:48:26.560
precisely she means.

00:48:26.560 --> 00:48:34.119
I think it's some notion of unifying the country,
but she doesn't want to move in that direction

00:48:34.119 --> 00:48:39.839
until she unifies the Democratic Party, or
at the very least gets the nomination.

00:48:39.839 --> 00:48:47.019
She will move the center, almost certainly,
if in fact she faces Cruz or Trump, as appears

00:48:47.019 --> 00:48:48.089
most likely.

00:48:48.089 --> 00:48:52.640
But what is very, very clear, and I'm curious
Scott and Kellyanne's comment, perhaps quickly

00:48:52.640 --> 00:48:56.319
because we're running out of time, do either
of you feel Hillary Clinton has a message?

00:48:56.319 --> 00:48:57.319
CONWAY: No.

00:48:57.319 --> 00:48:58.319
MILLER: No.

00:48:58.319 --> 00:48:59.380
CONWAY: And I don't say that as a partisan.

00:48:59.380 --> 00:49:00.839
I'm astonished she doesn't.

00:49:00.839 --> 00:49:02.959
She has very smart people working for her
because she's very smart.

00:49:02.959 --> 00:49:05.109
MILLER: No, it's she's going to win.

00:49:05.109 --> 00:49:06.109
It's get on the bandwagon.

00:49:06.109 --> 00:49:07.609
CONWAY: Well, that's not much of a message.

00:49:07.609 --> 00:49:08.609
MILLER: No, it's not.

00:49:08.609 --> 00:49:09.609
CONWAY: She tried that last night.

00:49:09.609 --> 00:49:10.609
(Laughs.)

00:49:10.609 --> 00:49:11.609
MILLER: No, I think it's not.

00:49:11.609 --> 00:49:12.609
STEIN: Ma'am.

00:49:12.609 --> 00:49:13.609
Q: My question-hi.

00:49:13.609 --> 00:49:14.609
I'm Nancy Lieberman.

00:49:14.609 --> 00:49:21.470
Here is my question: Bernie Sanders, it looks
like, is poised to win Wisconsin.

00:49:21.470 --> 00:49:27.390
And he has a week on the ground in New York-or
two weeks until the New York primary.

00:49:27.390 --> 00:49:33.519
And last night he had 18,000 people in the
South Bronx cheering him on, OK.

00:49:33.519 --> 00:49:39.979
So this is the scenario I'd like you to answer:
If, in fact, Bernie Sanders-despite what Mr.

00:49:39.979 --> 00:49:47.180
Miller said-somehow pulls it out and the superdelegates
realize that the voters don't want Hillary

00:49:47.180 --> 00:49:53.880
and they want Bernie, and it's Bernie versus
either Ted Cruz or Donald Trump, who for different

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:59.730
reasons I view as extreme in the other direction,
what do you think will happen in the presidential

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:05.160
election, and what will happen in the congressional
races, both for the House and Senate?

00:50:05.160 --> 00:50:07.259
STEIN: This is teed-up for you, Kellyanne.

00:50:07.259 --> 00:50:08.259
CONWAY: Yeah.

00:50:08.259 --> 00:50:09.259
(Laughter.)

00:50:09.259 --> 00:50:14.239
If Bernie-if my dreams come true and Bernie
Sanders is the Democratic nominee which, as

00:50:14.239 --> 00:50:17.680
you point out, is possible-folks, let's be
honest, if it were anybody other than Hillary

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:22.269
and Bernie people would be having a conversation
with the candidate that got 18 percent of

00:50:22.269 --> 00:50:28.249
the vote in Alaska, and 20-some percent in
Hawaii, and is now going to struggle more

00:50:28.249 --> 00:50:31.989
than she should have, break a sweat, in her
home state, if it's still her home state,

00:50:31.989 --> 00:50:35.880
of New York, and you know, against Bernie
Sanders.

00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:39.480
They'd be having a conversation with that
candidate to stand down.

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:44.660
If Bernie Sanders is the Democratic nominee,
I will personally go around this country and

00:50:44.660 --> 00:50:47.299
school everybody on what a socialist is.

00:50:47.299 --> 00:50:50.119
So we will call him socialist every single
day.

00:50:50.119 --> 00:50:52.979
He calls himself socialist every single day.

00:50:52.979 --> 00:50:58.029
And I think the Republicans will sweep because,
from a numbers perspective, there just aren't

00:50:58.029 --> 00:51:00.739
enough folks who will come out and vote for
him.

00:51:00.739 --> 00:51:08.289
And the Democratic Party is much more establishment,
much more-yes, establishment than I think

00:51:08.289 --> 00:51:13.690
people let on, since even Vice President Biden
didn't think there was necessarily a place

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:14.690
for him in the race.

00:51:14.690 --> 00:51:18.130
If he were running right now, he'd be running
away with it big time.

00:51:18.130 --> 00:51:20.019
He was the greatest fear that we had.

00:51:20.019 --> 00:51:23.680
But if it's Bernie Sanders-by the way, there
could be a revolt on the Democratic side.

00:51:23.680 --> 00:51:25.259
Everybody's talking about the convention in
Cleveland.

00:51:25.259 --> 00:51:29.029
Why not say, well, wait a second, the people
have spoken.

00:51:29.029 --> 00:51:32.979
Bernie Sanders has all these delegates, he
has all these votes, he has all this energy

00:51:32.979 --> 00:51:33.979
and momentum.

00:51:33.979 --> 00:51:38.160
Why are you going to upend the system with
the superdelegate situation?

00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:44.930
MILLER: Well, as you look at scenarios I wouldn't
stop with the scenario that Bernie Sanders

00:51:44.930 --> 00:51:46.130
may be the candidate.

00:51:46.130 --> 00:51:49.259
We may have three or four candidates.

00:51:49.259 --> 00:51:52.219
We may not have a winner of the electoral
college.

00:51:52.219 --> 00:51:55.660
We may have Paul Ryan as our president.

00:51:55.660 --> 00:52:03.289
I mean-so, I mean, really I do-I take seriously
what Caddell says, that you can bet on chaos.

00:52:03.289 --> 00:52:09.450
If Trump doesn't get the nomination, do you
have any doubt that he'll run as an independent,

00:52:09.450 --> 00:52:12.690
however crazy-
SCHOEN: I have doubts he'll run as an independent.

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:18.599
I have no doubt he and his supporters will
go right to the streets in Ohio and around

00:52:18.599 --> 00:52:19.670
the country.

00:52:19.670 --> 00:52:23.960
And the same thing could well happen at the
Democratic convention if what Kellyanne is

00:52:23.960 --> 00:52:29.010
saying plays out and Bernie's denied and he
has all these primary wins or near-wins, and

00:52:29.010 --> 00:52:31.809
his people feel cheated.

00:52:31.809 --> 00:52:33.380
STEIN: Albert.

00:52:33.380 --> 00:52:37.500
Q: Albert Knapp, NYU School of Medicine.

00:52:37.500 --> 00:52:40.509
The question for the panelists, we've talked
about some of the primaries.

00:52:40.509 --> 00:52:43.150
What about that special one looming, the FBI
primary?

00:52:43.150 --> 00:52:44.150
(Laughter.)

00:52:44.150 --> 00:52:46.849
CONWAY: They teed that up for you, Scott.

00:52:46.849 --> 00:52:47.849
(Laughter.)

00:52:47.849 --> 00:52:49.789
SCHOEN: Well, here's what I would say.

00:52:49.789 --> 00:52:56.420
And Kellyanne and I share this disability,
though she is as reluctant as I am to talk

00:52:56.420 --> 00:52:57.950
about it, so I apologize.

00:52:57.950 --> 00:52:59.960
I'm going to out you.

00:52:59.960 --> 00:53:01.259
You did go to law school once.

00:53:01.259 --> 00:53:02.259
CONWAY: I did.

00:53:02.259 --> 00:53:03.259
I'm a fully recovered attorney.

00:53:03.259 --> 00:53:04.259
(Laughter.)

00:53:04.259 --> 00:53:05.259
SCHOEN: And you did pass the bar, did you
not?

00:53:05.259 --> 00:53:06.259
CONWAY: Four of them.

00:53:06.259 --> 00:53:07.670
SCHOEN: And you did practice, did you not?

00:53:07.670 --> 00:53:08.690
CONWAY: I practiced.

00:53:08.690 --> 00:53:09.690
Guilty.

00:53:09.690 --> 00:53:10.690
(Laughs.)

00:53:10.690 --> 00:53:11.690
SCHOEN: OK.

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:12.690
Guilty as charged.

00:53:12.690 --> 00:53:13.690
OK.

00:53:13.690 --> 00:53:16.539
So we have two lawyers and one solid citizen.

00:53:16.539 --> 00:53:17.539
(Laughter.)

00:53:17.539 --> 00:53:19.099
And the person who put us together, Skip.

00:53:19.099 --> 00:53:20.099
(Laughter.)

00:53:20.099 --> 00:53:24.789
So here's what I would say: I've read the
statutes.

00:53:24.789 --> 00:53:31.269
And I would urge all of those who are interested
in this email matter that Dr. Knapp raises

00:53:31.269 --> 00:53:32.309
to read the statutes.

00:53:32.309 --> 00:53:34.069
There are two standards.

00:53:34.069 --> 00:53:41.240
One is knowingly transmitted classified information.

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:44.690
And the other is gross negligence in handling.

00:53:44.690 --> 00:53:50.980
And I think in my mind, having looked at the
precedent, read the statutes, and considered

00:53:50.980 --> 00:53:57.539
the facts, it's at least enough of an open
question to say that it is, certainly in my

00:53:57.539 --> 00:54:05.319
judgement, Marty, a condition precedent for
what Obama says or might say, or Valerie Jarrett

00:54:05.319 --> 00:54:10.170
says or might say to Loretta Lynch before
we get to the issue of what they're going

00:54:10.170 --> 00:54:16.009
to say in October on the campaign trail, because
it's pretty clear to me that somewhere before

00:54:16.009 --> 00:54:21.270
the end of June we've got to get some indication
of how this is going to be resolved.

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:29.450
And I think you can make pretty strong arguments
either way, and that it is not at all an obvious

00:54:29.450 --> 00:54:32.259
matter as to how it should be resolved.

00:54:32.259 --> 00:54:35.009
MILLER: Isn't there a royalty exemption, though,
for-

00:54:35.009 --> 00:54:36.009
SCHOEN: Royalty?

00:54:36.009 --> 00:54:38.749
MILLER: Yeah, that she gets out-
SCHOEN: She would like that.

00:54:38.749 --> 00:54:41.219
MILLER: I mean, does anybody-how many people
think she's going to be indicted?

00:54:41.219 --> 00:54:44.119
Yeah, that's what I would think.

00:54:44.119 --> 00:54:45.569
I don't either.

00:54:45.569 --> 00:54:47.259
Now, that's not the point.

00:54:47.259 --> 00:54:49.670
CONWAY: How many think she should?

00:54:49.670 --> 00:54:50.670
(Laughter.)

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:51.670
That's a good question, how many think she
should?

00:54:51.670 --> 00:54:53.199
MILLER: How many think she should?

00:54:53.199 --> 00:54:54.930
CONWAY: More hands, exactly.

00:54:54.930 --> 00:54:55.930
Yeah.

00:54:55.930 --> 00:54:56.930
MILLER: There you go.

00:54:56.930 --> 00:54:57.930
(Laughs.)

00:54:57.930 --> 00:54:58.930
STEIN: Zirin.

00:54:58.930 --> 00:55:05.150
Q: The subtext-the subtext of "Make America
Great Again," which is where Trump started

00:55:05.150 --> 00:55:10.049
out, is that somehow or other we've lost ground
during the eight years under Obama.

00:55:10.049 --> 00:55:14.680
We've lost ground internationally, we've lost
ground in prestige, we've lost ground for

00:55:14.680 --> 00:55:18.009
all the factors that Doug alluded to.

00:55:18.009 --> 00:55:25.259
So now we're confronted with a situation where
Obama's approval rating is higher than it's

00:55:25.259 --> 00:55:32.880
ever been in the time that he's been in office,
and that he's not a negative to a Democratic

00:55:32.880 --> 00:55:33.880
candidate.

00:55:33.880 --> 00:55:36.779
As Kellyanne suggested, he's a big positive.

00:55:36.779 --> 00:55:42.480
And if he campaigns with Hillary, that could
certainly tip the scale if it's at all a close

00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:43.480
election.

00:55:43.480 --> 00:55:47.979
But I wondered what your thoughts were about
this elephant in the room, Barack Obama, who

00:55:47.979 --> 00:55:50.130
is kind of the third candidate.

00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:56.109
It started out that people would vote Republican
because they wanted to vote against him, and

00:55:56.109 --> 00:56:03.440
now-and how does that play out with your scenario
of anger against the system, when the system

00:56:03.440 --> 00:56:06.079
seems to be something that's supported by
a large number of people?

00:56:06.079 --> 00:56:10.440
MILLER: Well, Kellyanne-yeah, she made a great
point about the power that he may wield in

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:11.440
this campaign.

00:56:11.440 --> 00:56:16.839
One of the things I had hoped to un-elect
him in 2012.

00:56:16.839 --> 00:56:21.859
And when we were doing the research on that,
on the narrative that might move 6 million

00:56:21.859 --> 00:56:25.451
voters to change their votes, they would go
through-all those voters would go through

00:56:25.451 --> 00:56:27.670
a litany and they would say: Look, he's a
good man.

00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:29.130
He has good values.

00:56:29.130 --> 00:56:30.960
He has-you know, he's smart.

00:56:30.960 --> 00:56:32.500
He's doing the best he can.

00:56:32.500 --> 00:56:34.599
He's being opposed at every turn.

00:56:34.599 --> 00:56:37.039
And he inherited a mess.

00:56:37.039 --> 00:56:43.930
Now that he's in the, you know, last bit of
his tenure, I think those things are rising

00:56:43.930 --> 00:56:46.099
in people's estimation of him.

00:56:46.099 --> 00:56:53.690
This is a good guy, he goes home to a family,
he has dinner with his kids, he's an exemplary

00:56:53.690 --> 00:56:54.770
example.

00:56:54.770 --> 00:56:55.859
He's just not a leader.

00:56:55.859 --> 00:56:57.710
But that-I mean, that's my opinion.

00:56:57.710 --> 00:56:59.270
And that's the people's opinion.

00:56:59.270 --> 00:57:01.490
But he's not going to be a leader either.

00:57:01.490 --> 00:57:06.709
So now they can sort of remove that issue
about whether he's a leader or not and sort

00:57:06.709 --> 00:57:07.950
of look at the personal issues.

00:57:07.950 --> 00:57:09.740
And I think that's what's rising.

00:57:09.740 --> 00:57:14.019
CONWAY: Well, yes, he seems to be a wonderful
husband and father.

00:57:14.019 --> 00:57:17.729
But you said he goes home to dinner with his
family.

00:57:17.729 --> 00:57:21.819
He also does the wave in Cuba and then the
Tango in Argentina while the world is burning.

00:57:21.819 --> 00:57:23.569
And people-that costs him.

00:57:23.569 --> 00:57:26.579
People did not like those reactions and you
all know it.

00:57:26.579 --> 00:57:32.200
If you read something other than the hometown
newspapers, that was not the most appropriate

00:57:32.200 --> 00:57:34.539
reaction, according to many Americans.

00:57:34.539 --> 00:57:39.589
Having said that, as the person raised the
specter of how helpful President Obama will

00:57:39.589 --> 00:57:43.549
be to Secretary Clinton, it's also going to
be difficult for her because it's going to

00:57:43.549 --> 00:57:48.680
remind many voters why they chose him over
her eight years ago.

00:57:48.680 --> 00:57:51.420
And it's not a good contrast for her, necessarily.

00:57:51.420 --> 00:57:56.720
My view is, she's been trying from the beginning
to figure out how to run for both the third

00:57:56.720 --> 00:58:02.190
term of Bill Clinton and the third term of
Barack Obama.

00:58:02.190 --> 00:58:03.190
(Laughter.)

00:58:03.190 --> 00:58:07.160
And that's ironic in and of itself, for those
feminists in the audience, it's ironic in

00:58:07.160 --> 00:58:08.369
and of itself.

00:58:08.369 --> 00:58:12.900
But it's-but either one has a critical mass
of support.

00:58:12.900 --> 00:58:15.479
There's not enough voters saying: I want the
third term of Bill Clinton.

00:58:15.479 --> 00:58:19.579
Plenty of people say I loved the economy,
I loved the peace and prosperity, but it's

00:58:19.579 --> 00:58:20.579
not enough.

00:58:20.579 --> 00:58:22.279
In other words, there are not enough voters.

00:58:22.279 --> 00:58:26.579
And then there certainly aren't enough voters
in the general election to say: I want year

00:58:26.579 --> 00:58:28.789
nine of Obama/Biden to start now.

00:58:28.789 --> 00:58:31.630
If there were, I assume Biden would have run
for year nine of Obama/Biden.

00:58:31.630 --> 00:58:35.569
But that aside, it still is very difficult
to win that third term.

00:58:35.569 --> 00:58:40.049
It's very difficult of the party in power
to get that third term.

00:58:40.049 --> 00:58:43.499
I think he's a net positive for Secretary
Clinton.

00:58:43.499 --> 00:58:46.390
And I predict he will be much more active
for her.

00:58:46.390 --> 00:58:50.200
He's got to watch his left flank, though,
President Obama, because these Bernie Sanders

00:58:50.200 --> 00:58:55.079
voters, many of them supported Barack Obama
over Hillary Clinton eight years ago.

00:58:55.079 --> 00:59:00.039
So he has to be very careful not to offend-not
just Senator Sanders, but more importantly

00:59:00.039 --> 00:59:05.799
not to offend the Sanders voters, just as
the Republicans need to-if they want to criticize

00:59:05.799 --> 00:59:09.140
Donald Trump it's one thing, but you have
to study his voters.

00:59:09.140 --> 00:59:14.040
What is motivating and animating them to stand
in line for two hours just to vote?

00:59:14.040 --> 00:59:20.039
If he's not the eventual nominee, how do you
dispense with him if he's not the nominee

00:59:20.039 --> 00:59:22.609
and yet still attract his voters?

00:59:22.609 --> 00:59:28.959
STEIN: So we could go on for a lot longer,
but it is 9:00 and closing time.

00:59:28.959 --> 00:59:30.809
Thank you.

00:59:30.809 --> 00:59:35.440
Thanks for doing this session.

00:59:35.440 --> 00:59:36.440
(Applause.)

00:59:36.440 --> 00:59:37.289
(END)

